# Why TypeScript? 🛡️

In plain JavaScript, variables can silently change types, leading to catastrophic runtime errors:

```javascript_not
// Plain JavaScript: No error until user runs code!
let user = { name: "Alice" };
user.toUppercase(); // TypeError: user.toUppercase is not a function (Typo in 'C'!)
```

## The TypeScript Solution:
In TypeScript, errors are caught **immediately at compile time** before your code ever runs in production:

```typescript
let username: string = "Alice";
let age: number = 28;
let isSubscribed: boolean = true;

console.log(`User: ${username}, Age: ${age}, Active: ${isSubscribed}`);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let username: string = "Alice";
let age: number = 28;
let isSubscribed: boolean = true;

console.log(`User: ${username}, Age: ${age}, Active: ${isSubscribed}`);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# The Core Primitive Types 🏷️

TypeScript provides explicit annotations for all JavaScript primitives:

```typescript
// Explicit type annotations
const siteName: string = "CodeCerebrum";
const rating: number = 4.9;
const isVip: boolean = true;
const bigId: bigint = 9007199254740991n;
const uniqueKey: symbol = Symbol("apiKey");

console.log(siteName, rating, isVip);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const siteName: string = "CodeCerebrum";
const rating: number = 4.9;
const isVip: boolean = true;

console.log("Site:", siteName);
console.log("Rating:", rating);
console.log("Is VIP:", isVip);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 💡 **Notice:** `number` covers both integers and floating-point numbers.

# Type Inference: Less Code, Same Safety 🧠

You don't need to manually type everything. If you assign a value upon declaration, TypeScript **infers** the type automatically!

```typescript
// TypeScript infers 'count' is a number
let count = 42;

// count = "forty-two"; // Error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'!
count += 8;
console.log("Count:", count);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let count = 42;
count += 8;
console.log("Inferred number count:", count);

let message = "TypeScript is awesome";
console.log("Inferred string uppercase:", message.toUpperCase());
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 🎯 **Best Practice:** Let TypeScript infer simple primitive variables. Add explicit annotations when variables are initialized later or when defining function signatures!

# The `any` Type: Disabling Safety ⚠️

`any` disables all type checking for a variable, effectively turning TypeScript back into plain JavaScript:

```typescript
let wildcard: any = "Hello";
wildcard = 100;      // Allowed
wildcard = [1, 2];   // Allowed
// wildcard.nonExistentMethod(); // No compile error, but will CRASH at runtime!
console.log("Wildcard value:", wildcard);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let wildcard: any = "Hello";
wildcard = 100;
wildcard = { score: 99 };
console.log("Wildcard score:", wildcard.score);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 🚨 **Warning:** Avoid `any`! Overusing `any` undermines the entire reason for using TypeScript. Enable `"noImplicitAny": true` in your `tsconfig.json`.

# The `unknown` Type: Type-Safe Alternative 🦺

`unknown` represents any value, but unlike `any`, TypeScript **will not let you perform operations on it** until you narrow its type first!

```typescript
let userInput: unknown;
userInput = "San Francisco";

// ❌ Error: Property 'toUpperCase' does not exist on type 'unknown'
// console.log(userInput.toUpperCase());

// ✅ Safe: Type check (Type Narrowing) first!
if (typeof userInput === "string") {
  console.log("Uppercased:", userInput.toUpperCase());
}
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let userInput: unknown = "San Francisco";

if (typeof userInput === "string") {
  console.log("Narrowed unknown to string:", userInput.toUpperCase());
}
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Typed Arrays 📚

Declare arrays that strictly enforce the types of their elements:

```typescript
// Two equivalent syntaxes:
const scores: number[] = [95, 82, 88];
const cities: Array<string> = ["Tokyo", "London", "Paris"];

scores.push(91); // Valid
// scores.push("invalid"); // Compile Error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to 'number'

console.log("Scores:", scores);
console.log("Cities:", cities);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const scores: number[] = [95, 82, 88];
const cities: Array<string> = ["Tokyo", "London", "Paris"];

scores.push(91);
console.log("Scores array:", scores);
console.log("Cities array:", cities);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Tuples: Fixed Length & Ordered Types 📦

A **Tuple** is an array with a fixed number of elements where each position has a specific type:

```typescript
// A tuple of [string, number] representing a key-value pair
let httpResponse: [string, number];

httpResponse = ["OK", 200]; // Valid!
// httpResponse = [200, "OK"]; // Error: Types must match exact position!

console.log(`Status: ${httpResponse[0]} (Code: ${httpResponse[1]})`);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let httpResponse: [string, number] = ["OK", 200];
console.log(`Status: ${httpResponse[0]} (Code: ${httpResponse[1]})`);

// Destructuring tuples
const [statusText, statusCode] = httpResponse;
console.log(`Destructured -> Text: ${statusText}, Code: ${statusCode}`);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# `null` & `undefined` with `strictNullChecks` ❓

In modern TypeScript projects, `"strictNullChecks": true` is enabled by default. This prevents variables from holding `null` or `undefined` unless explicitly declared as a union!

```typescript
let activeUser: string | null = "Alice";

activeUser = null; // Valid because of union type!

function printLength(text: string | null) {
  if (text !== null) {
    console.log("Length:", text.length); // Safe!
  } else {
    console.log("Text is null");
  }
}

printLength("Hello");
printLength(null);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let activeUser: string | null = "Alice";
activeUser = null;

function printLength(text: string | null) {
  if (text !== null) {
    console.log("Length:", text.length);
  } else {
    console.log("Text is null");
  }
}

printLength("Hello");
printLength(null);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Type Assertions (`as Type`) 🔀

When you know more specific information about a value's type than TypeScript does, use a **type assertion**:

```typescript
const rawValue: unknown = "Hello TypeScript";

// Assert that rawValue is a string
const strLength = (rawValue as string).length;
console.log("Asserted string length:", strLength);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const rawValue: unknown = "Hello TypeScript";
const strLength = (rawValue as string).length;
console.log("Asserted string length:", strLength);

// Angle-bracket syntax (alternative)
const strLength2 = (<string>rawValue).length;
console.log("Angle-bracket length:", strLength2);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> ⚠️ **Assertion Rule:** Type assertions do **not** perform runtime casting or data conversion. They are purely compile-time hints for the TypeScript type checker!

# The Non-Null Assertion Operator (`!`) ❗

Placing an exclamation mark `!` after an expression asserts that the value is definitely **neither `null` nor `undefined`**:

```typescript
function findElement(id: string): string | null {
  return id === "main" ? "Container Element" : null;
}

// Without !, TypeScript warns that element might be null
const element = findElement("main")!; // We assert it's not null!
console.log("Element length:", element.length);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
function findElement(id: string): string | null {
  return id === "main" ? "Container Element" : null;
}

const element = findElement("main")!;
console.log("Element length:", element.length);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> ⚠️ **Caution:** Use `!` only when you are 100% certain the value cannot be null at runtime!

# Readonly Arrays (`ReadonlyArray<T>`) 🔒

Prevent any mutation of an array after creation:

```typescript
const constantNumbers: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// constantNumbers.push(5); // Error: Property 'push' does not exist on type 'readonly number[]'
// constantNumbers[0] = 99; // Error: Index signature in type 'readonly number[]' only permits reading

console.log("Readonly array:", constantNumbers);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const constantNumbers: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
console.log("Readonly array elements:", constantNumbers.join(", "));
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Literal Types 🎯

A literal type represents an exact, specific value rather than any general string or number:

```typescript
// Only these 3 exact strings are allowed!
let direction: "NORTH" | "SOUTH" | "EAST" | "WEST";

direction = "NORTH"; // Valid
// direction = "UP"; // Error: Type '"UP"' is not assignable to type '"NORTH" | "SOUTH" | "EAST" | "WEST"'

console.log("Valid direction:", direction);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let direction: "NORTH" | "SOUTH" | "EAST" | "WEST" = "NORTH";
console.log("Current direction:", direction);

let statusCode: 200 | 404 | 500 = 200;
console.log("Current HTTP code:", statusCode);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Union Types (`|`) 🔀

Allow a variable to accept one of several types using the pipe operator `|`:

```typescript
let id: string | number;

id = 101;     // Valid!
console.log("Numeric ID:", id);

id = "USR-204"; // Valid!
console.log("String ID:", id);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
let id: string | number;
id = 101;
console.log("Numeric ID:", id);

id = "USR-204";
console.log("String ID:", id);

function formatId(identifier: string | number): string {
  return `ID_${identifier}`;
}
console.log("Formatted:", formatId(55));
console.log("Formatted:", formatId("custom"));
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Type Narrowing with `typeof` 🔍

When a variable is a union (e.g. `string | number`), use `typeof` checks to narrow it down so TypeScript knows which methods are safe to call:

```typescript
function processValue(val: string | number) {
  if (typeof val === "string") {
    // Inside this block, TypeScript KNOWS val is a string!
    console.log("Uppercase string:", val.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    // Inside this block, TypeScript KNOWS val is a number!
    console.log("Fixed number:", val.toFixed(2));
  }
}

processValue("developer");
processValue(42.5678);
```

